{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgQ31BZfQhLoFCKkm1vea","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Oracle's effective tax rate for the fiscal year ended 31 May 2024 was **10.9%**, up from 6.8% the year before, an increase of 410bps.\n\n**Effective Tax Rate (FY ended 5/31/2024)**\n- FY2024: **10.9%**\n- FY2023: **6.8%**\n- Delta: **+410bps**\n\n**Income Tax Expense (Provision)**\n- FY2024: **$1,274M**\n- FY2023: **$623M**\n- Change: +$651M, which is about 2.0x the prior year\n\nThe tax expense roughly doubled, while the rate rose by about 4.1 points. That means pre-tax income grew much more slowly than the tax charge. The data I pulled didn't include Oracle's pre-tax income for these years, so it isn't shown. The XBRL data doesn't explain why the rate went up; the tax note in the 10-K would.\n\nLater Oracle 10-Ks report the same FY2024 and FY2023 figures. Those filings are dated after 16 May 2025, so I didn't use them.\n\n**Sources**\n- Oracle Corp. Form 10-K, filed 2024-06-20, accession 0000950170-24-075605: FY2024 and FY2023 effective tax rate and income tax expense.\n- Oracle Corp. Form 10-K, filed 2023-06-20, accession 0000950170-23-028914: confirms the FY2023 figures (6.8% rate, $623M expense)."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5671,"cache_read_input_tokens":5780,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5671},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"45a26c0a-3a96-4b7e-8dda-bad7778bd868","uuid":"a047028e-26c3-4320-9721-af42900cff77","timestamp":"2026-10-26T02:09:55.544Z","request_id":"req_011CgQ31BJHstFuXbJRBtb4H"}
